title: Read from narrow to broad
pid: 6c499fdc-e718-4331-96ec-c9f7c2ff3ef7
body: |2-
  To find the file that does something, descend by headers before
  grepping: two or three short reads reach it. Every module of
  =server/= opens with a =//!= header saying what it is for and listing
  its children, and =server/lib.rs= lists the top-level modules by what
  a user does. The clients' files open with headers likewise, indexed
  by =elisp/skg-init.el= and by "Where things live" in
  =nvim/README.org=; =tools/skg-for-ai/skg_for_ai.py= indexes the
  modules beside it. Whoever moves, splits or adds a file keeps its
  header and its parent's list true, by the conventions in
  =TODO/refactor-for-dev/module-headers.org=: a stale list sends the
  next reader to the wrong file.

  Then use =rg= to locate definitions and callers, and read the relevant
  ranges and follow the call graph as correctness requires.  Let an exact
  symbol, wire field, invariant, or failing test define the initial question;
  avoid rereading large files or diffs when the relevant region has not changed.
  This is a discovery order, not permission to ignore surrounding invariants.
  The repository's
  =tools/introspect/call-graph=, =change-forest=, and =code-map= can help when the
  ordinary text search stops being clear.
